###### Abstract

The STAR experiment analyzes the results of nucleus-nucleus collisions at the RHIC facility at Brookhaven National Laboratory. The STAR detector is mainly composed of a solenoidal Time Projection Chamber that was designed to provide wide coverage of the resulting outgoing particles. Over a 24-hour period on November 25th and 26th of 2001, the RHIC facility ran a Au + Au collider beam with a center of mass energy of 19.6 GeV per nucleon-nucleon pair. This dissertation examines directed and elliptic flow measurements at this energy and compares the results to experiments run at similar energies. A discussion of the analysis methods, along with the event and track quality requirements, are included in this paper.

